From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: amdv: hold VS10-forced HDR10 across the SDR->HDR switch

When VS10 converts a Dolby Vision source to HDR10 output
(dolby_vision_policy == AMDV_FORCE_OUTPUT_MODE, force_mode == HDR10), the
policy process gates HDR10 on sink_support_hdr(vinfo). That helper applies
the amvecm 8-bit HDR10 limitation: when the CURRENT hdmi output colordepth
is 8-bit it strips HDR_SUPPORT. During the SDR->HDR mode switch the output
is transiently still the outgoing 8-bit depth, so a mid-switch re-eval (an
FEL 'not found el' frame at clip start, or any per-frame event for profile
8) sees no HDR support and forces SDR8 - itself 8-bit, so it never climbs to
12-bit HDR10 and latches on SDR. Native HDR10 escapes only because it is
already 12-bit.

Key the forced-HDR10 decision on the sink's real HDR capability
(sink_hdr_support_ori_cap, the un-8bit-limited cap - the panel's EDID
advertises SMPTE ST 2084) instead of the transient output depth, so it holds
HDR10 through the switch and completes to 12-bit like native HDR10. Applied
to both the single-core (v1) and multi-core STB (v2_stb) force-HDR10 gates;
the global sink_hdr_support and its 8-bit protection are untouched.

--- a/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
@@ -4282,7 +4282,7 @@
 					src_format, dolby_vision_mode, *mode);
 				}
 		} else if (force_mode == AMDV_OUTPUT_MODE_HDR10) {
-			if ((vinfo && sink_support_hdr(vinfo)) ||
+			if ((vinfo && (sink_hdr_support_ori_cap(vinfo) & HDR_SUPPORT)) ||
 				debug_force_mode) {
 				*mode = AMDV_OUTPUT_MODE_HDR10;
 				if (dolby_vision_mode != *mode)
@@ -4758,7 +4758,7 @@
 					src_format, dolby_vision_mode, *mode);
 				}
 		} else if (force_mode == AMDV_OUTPUT_MODE_HDR10) {
-			if ((vinfo && sink_support_hdr(vinfo)) ||
+			if ((vinfo && (sink_hdr_support_ori_cap(vinfo) & HDR_SUPPORT)) ||
 				debug_force_mode) {
 				*mode = AMDV_OUTPUT_MODE_HDR10;
 				if (dolby_vision_mode != *mode)
